      "wernicke_area_stimulation_disrupts_lan": "Wernicke-area stimulation disrupts language COMPREHENSION and produces jargon, unlike the clean arrest from Broca area.",
      "what_does_the_awake_patient_experience": "What does the awake patient experience?",
      "what_happens_when_this_region_is_damag": "What happens when this region is damaged?",
      "what_is_forming": "What is forming",
      "what_states_would_you_not_expect_to_se": "What states would you NOT expect to see in this five-axis space, and what would they require?",
      "when_stimulation_reaches_threshold_55m": "When stimulation reaches threshold (-55mV), voltage-gated Na⁺ channels open and the neuron fires at FULL strength. There is no “half” signal — it either fires completely or not at all. Stronger stimuli increase firing RATE, not intensity.",
      "which_region_was_stimulated": "Which region was stimulated?",
      "why_is_the_low_serotonin_depression_cl": "Why is the \"low serotonin → depression\" claim contested in current research?",
      "wilder_penfield_mapped_the_brain_by_ge": "Wilder Penfield mapped the brain by gently stimulating awake patients during surgery. Predict what the patient would experience.",
      "with_repeated_exposure_postsynaptic_re": "With repeated exposure, postsynaptic receptors undergo downregulation — the cell reduces receptor density or sensitivity (internalization) to compensate for excess stimulation. This means higher doses are needed to achieve the same effect, driving the cycle of tolerance and dependence. Abrupt cessation can cause withdrawal as the nervous system has adapted to the drug’s presence.",
      "your_hypothesis_which_two_neurotransmi": "Your hypothesis (which two neurotransmitters most strongly trade off in shaping affect?)",
