      "reversing_the_life_cycle_medusae_trans": "Reversing the life cycle: medusae transforming into polyps and cell transdifferentiation in Turritopsis nutricula",
      "review_of_evidence_of_sentience_in_cep": "Review of evidence of sentience in cephalopod molluscs and decapod crustaceans",
      "review_of_the_evidence_of_sentience_in": "Review of the evidence of sentience in cephalopod molluscs and decapod crustaceans",
      "rna_editing_in_cephalopod_nervous_syst": "RNA editing in cephalopod nervous systems",
      "rna_editing_is_the_process_of_altering": "RNA editing is the process of altering RNA after it's been transcribed from DNA, before it's translated into protein. Most animals do some RNA editing, but at low levels (~3% of transcripts in mammals). Cephalopods are RADICALLY different — Liscovitch-Brauer et al. 2017 (Cell) documented that ~60% of cephalopod neuronal transcripts are RNA-edited (adenosine-to-inosine recoding).\n\nWhat does this do? Each editing event can change a single amino acid in the resulting protein, OR introduce an early stop codon, OR change protein folding behavior. In aggregate, RNA editing dramatically expands the proteins a cell can make from one DNA sequence. It's essentially a SECOND CODE laid on top of the DNA code.\n\nWhy do cephalopods do so much? Two hypotheses are leading:\n\nHypothesis 1 — Temperature adaptation. RNA editing levels respond to temperature changes. Cephalopod neurons may use RNA editing to adjust protein function across the temperature range their environment offers. Liscovitch-Brauer + others have documented temperature-correlated editing patterns.\n\nHypothesis 2 — Neural plasticity. Cephalopod brains need to be plastic enough for the kinds of rapid learning these animals do (jar-opening, mimicry, etc.). RNA editing provides a mechanism for changing neural protein behavior rapidly without altering DNA. This might explain the cognitive flexibility cephalopods are known for.\n\nA TRADE-OFF: Liscovitch-Brauer showed that the increased RNA editing comes at the cost of REDUCED genome evolution. Sites under heavy RNA editing pressure have lower mutation rates over evolutionary time. The cephalopod genome may have \"frozen\" partially to accommodate the editing system.\n\nThis is genuinely unprecedented. Among invertebrates, this level of RNA editing is unique to coleoid cephalopods (octopuses + squids + cuttlefish). Among vertebrates, it does not exist at this scale. RNA editing in cephalopods may inform our understanding of how nervous systems can adapt to dynamic environments — and what alternative cognitive architectures might look like.\n\n(Source: Liscovitch-Brauer, N., et al. (2017). Trade-off between transcriptome plasticity and genome evolution in cephalopods. Cell.)",
      "roberts_2002_trends_ecol_evol": "Roberts, 2002 — Trends Ecol. Evol.",
